course: english
lesson: 1
title: Sounds and letters: how phonics starts
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Every letter has a name and a sound. The name is what you sing in the alphabet song («ess», «bee», «see»); the sound is what the letter says inside a word (/s/, /b/, /k/). When Miss Adebayo holds up the snake card and hisses «sssss», the snake is showing the sound; «ess» is only the letter's name.
explain: Reading starts from the sounds, because sounds glue together and names do not. Say /k/ /a/ /t/ faster and faster and it becomes «cat»; say «see-ay-tee» as fast as you like and it never turns into «cat». Gluing sounds into a word is called blending.
explain: English has 26 letters but about 44 sounds (the exact number changes a little with accent), so some sounds need a team of letters. «sh» in «ship» is two letters making one sound, a digraph; «igh» in «night» is three letters making one sound, a trigraph. Ruby thought that was cheating, but the letters are simply working as a team.
explain: Schools teach the sounds in a planned order, in stages called phases or sets. First come s, a, t, p, i, n, because with just those six a child can already read «sat», «pin», «tap» and «nap». Made-up «alien words» like «zib» are read on purpose: they show a child is really reading the sounds and not guessing a word they remember.
explain: People learn and speak differently and are still right. Mum in London and Mom in Texas spell that word their own country's way; a girl who speaks Arabic at home learns English sounds on top; Grandad in Glasgow, who learned whole words by their shape, can still help by reading stories aloud. And a big sister who calls phonics «mid» (teen slang for boring) used it herself, and still does, fast and silently.
fact: Every letter has a name (ay, bee, see) and a sound (/a/, /b/, /k/); for reading, the sound comes first.
fact: Blending means pushing short, clipped sounds together to make a word: /k/ /a/ /t/ makes «cat»; letter names do not blend, and «suh-tuh» sounds add extra bits.
fact: A phoneme is one sound in a word; a grapheme is the letter or letters that write that sound.
fact: English has 26 letters but about 44 sounds, so some sounds are written with letter teams.
fact: A digraph is two letters making one sound (sh, ch, th, ng, and ee in «sheep»); a trigraph is three letters making one sound (igh, air).
fact: «th» has two sounds: buzzy in «this», where your throat hums, and quiet in «thin», just air; some accents say «fink» and «dis», but the spelling is still th.
fact: Hearing the separate sounds in a word, such as the first sound /m/ in «moon», comes before matching sounds to letters; «I spy» with sounds trains it.
fact: Schools teach sounds in a fixed order (phases or sets), starting with s, a, t, p, i, n, so children can read real words from the first week.
fact: Reading made-up «alien words» such as «zib» proves a child is decoding the sounds, not guessing from memory.
fact: Phonics is teaching reading by matching sounds to letters; many grown-ups learned whole words by their shape instead, but phonics lets a child work out words they have never seen.
fact: Fluent readers still use phonics, fast and silently, and slow down to it when they meet a hard new word.
fact: Children who speak another language at home sort out the sounds fine; a home language does not hold back English reading.
fact: «Mum» (Britain) and «mom» (USA) are both correct spellings in their own country.
fact: Slang in this lesson: «mid» means boring or average; «wean» is Scottish for a small child; «sweet as» is New Zealand and Australian for great; «our kid» is a family word in Liverpool and Manchester; «y'all» is «you all» in the US South.
exercise: Miss Adebayo hisses «sssss» at the snake card and Leo shouts «Ess!». What has Leo told her? | The letter's name, not the sound it makes in words | The sound the letter makes in words | The name of the snake on Miss Adebayo's picture card | A different letter that sounds very much like s
exercise: Ava's mum says «see-ay-tee» and Ava cannot hear «cat». Why not? | Letter names never glue together into the word | Ava is too young to read three-letter words yet | Mum said the letters in the wrong order | The word «cat» has a silent letter in it
exercise: Grandad in Glasgow calls his granddaughter a «wean». What is a wean? | A small child | A school teacher | A new reading book that schools use for phonics | A letter of the alphabet
exercise: Ruby says «sh» in «ship» should be two sounds. What is it really? | Two letters making one sound, a digraph | Two sounds said very fast together | A spelling mistake that the book printer should fix | A silent s followed by an h sound
exercise: How many letters and about how many sounds does English have? | 26 letters and about 44 sounds | 26 letters and exactly 26 sounds | 44 letters and about 26 sounds | 26 letters and over 200 sounds
exercise: Kai puts his hand on his throat and says «this», then «thin». What does he notice? | His throat hums in «this» but not in «thin» | His throat hums in both words the same | «thin» hums but «this» does not hum | Neither word makes any sound in his throat
exercise: Jayden's sister says phonics is «mid». What does «mid» mean? | Boring or average | Very difficult | In the middle of the book | Brilliant, really good
exercise: The teacher asks for a word beginning with /m/ and Ollie says «Ollie!». What is Ollie still learning? | To hear the first sound inside a word | To write the letter m neatly on the line | His own name | To count how many letters are in the alphabet
exercise: Why do many schools teach s, a, t, p, i, n first? | With just six sounds you can read many real words | They are the first six letters of the alphabet | They are the hardest sounds, so they go first | They are the only sounds that appear in short English words
exercise: Mia reads the made-up word «zib». Dad says it is not real. Who is right about her reading? | Mia: she sounded it out correctly | Dad: made-up words cannot be read at all | Dad: she must have guessed it | Neither of them, «zib» is a real word
exercise: Amira speaks Arabic at home. What does that do to her English phonics? | Nothing bad; she can learn the English sounds on top | It stops her learning English sounds at all | It means she has to stop speaking Arabic first | It makes her mix up every English letter
exercise: Finn says «mom», his London cousin says «mum». Which spelling is right? | Both, each in its own country | Only «mom», because America is bigger | Only «mum», because English came from England | Neither; the word is spelled «mam»
exercise: Nan in Liverpool says phonics phases are like the Football League. What are phases? | Stages in a planned order of teaching sounds | Teams of children who play sports at school | Tests that decide which class or group a new child goes into | The pages in a reading book, numbered
exercise: What is a grapheme? | The letter or letters that write one sound | One sound you hear in a spoken word | A word you can read straight away by its shape | A picture card the teacher uses
exercise: Which word has a trigraph? | night | ship | cat | sheep
exercise: How should you say the sound of s when you blend? | Short and clipped: /s/ | Long with an extra bit: «suh» | By its name: «ess» | Loud and slowly: «sssuh-uh»
test: «my nan says u gotta learn the whole alphabet song before u can read, that's the rule innit». Is she right? | No, the song helps with order, but reading starts from the sounds | Yes, nobody can read a word until they can sing the whole song | Yes, but only the first half of the song | No, the song is useless and nobody should sing it
test: Big sis: «phonics is so mid, real readers don't use it, they just know the words». Put her right. | Real readers use phonics fast and silently, and slow down to it on new words | She's right, grown-up readers have never used phonics in their lives | She's right, phonics only works for words with three letters | Real readers only use phonics when they read out loud to a class, never in their heads
test: Ollie said «Ollie!» for /m/ and the whole class laughed. What is the wise move for the teacher? | Play «I spy» with sounds until hearing first sounds clicks | Laugh too, so Ollie learns to stop guessing | Tell Ollie he is not ready for school yet | Make Ollie copy the letter m fifty times
test: What would happen if every English sound had to be written with one letter only? | We would need more letters than the 26 we have | Nothing, 26 letters cover every sound already | We could drop half the alphabet | All words would become exactly three letters long
test: Dad in the car: «zib? that's not a word, try again». Mia read it perfectly. What could he say instead? | «Brilliant sounding out! It's an alien word» | «Good guess, but guessing is how reading works» | «Only real words count, so it was wrong» | «You need to learn that word by its shape»
test: Kai in Auckland: «Mum says the th in thin is buzzy, sweet as». Spot the slip. | Thin is the quiet th; this is the buzzy one | No slip, thin is the buzzy th | Th always makes exactly one sound | Thin has no th sound at all, only t
test: Auntie: «if Amira keeps chattin' Arabic at home she'll never read English proper». What is true? | Speaking a home language does not hold back English reading | She's right, children can only learn one language | Amira must stop speaking Arabic before she reads | Amira will read English but never be able to spell it
test: Grandad: «nae fancy phonics in ma day, we just read». What is fair to say? | Many learned by whole words, but phonics helps children read words they've never seen | Phonics was used by everyone in his day, he just forgot | Whole-word reading is the only way anyone has ever learned to read, in any country | Grandad couldn't have learned to read at all without phonics
test: In a cartoon, a talking letter S says its name, «ESS!», and the word «sat» appears. What is wrong? | The sound /s/ is what goes into «sat», not the name | Nothing, letter names build words in real reading | S should say «suh-uh» for the word to work | The word should be «ess-at», spelled with four letters
test: ★★★ Ruby counts letters and sounds in «sheep». How many letters, and how many sounds? | 5 letters, 3 sounds (sh, ee, p) | 5 letters, 5 sounds (s, h, e, e, p) | 3 letters, 5 sounds | 5 letters, 4 sounds (sh, e, e, p)
test: ★★★ Which made-up word could a child read with ONLY the first six sounds, s, a, t, p, i, n? | «pas» | «zib» | «shap» | «mot»
test: ★★★ Tom says: «light has five letters, so it has five sounds». How many sounds does «light» really have? | 3: l, igh, t | 5: l, i, g, h, t | 4: l, i, gh, t | 2: li, ght
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the sounds of letters, rather than their names, are employed when a child first learns to read. | The sounds may be blended into words, whereas the names may not | The names of letters are no longer taught in any school | The sounds are more numerous and therefore more interesting | The names of the letters vary from one country to another
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a digraph? | Two letters which together represent a single sound | A single letter which represents two separate sounds | Two words which are pronounced in an identical manner | A letter which is written but not pronounced at all
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the reading of pseudo-words by young pupils. | It demonstrates that the pupil decodes the sounds rather than recalling familiar words | It demonstrates that the pupil has failed to learn the vocabulary of the language | It is prohibited in schools, as it confuses the learner | It has no bearing whatsoever upon the assessment of reading, being merely a game for amusement
exam: It is given that a child speaks Arabic in the home and English at school. Ascertain the effect of the home language upon her acquisition of English phonics. | It does not impede her acquisition of English sounds | It renders the acquisition of English sounds impossible | It obliges her to abandon the home language entirely | It causes every English letter to be confused with another
